The courses in src/vocabbot/data/courses are licensed under CC BY-SA 4.0
(https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/). tools/courses/pipeline.py
builds them from:

- The CEFR-J Wordlist Version 1.5, compiled by Yukio Tono, Tokyo University
  of Foreign Studies (https://github.com/openlanguageprofiles/olp-en-cefrj).
  The CEFR levels of the concepts come from this list, which may be used for
  research and commercial purposes provided that it is cited.
- English Wiktionary (https://en.wiktionary.org), CC BY-SA 4.0 and GFDL, in
  the extract of wiktextract by Tatu Ylonen (https://kaikki.org/dictionary/),
  dump of 2026-10-01: translations, gender, forms and pronunciation.
- wordfreq 3.1.1 by Robyn Speer (https://github.com/rspeer/wordfreq), data
  under CC BY-SA 4.0: the order of the words and the choice of equivalents.
- Tatoeba (https://tatoeba.org), CC BY 2.0 FR: example sentences and their
  Polish translations. src/vocabbot/data/courses/<lang>/sentences.tsv names
  the author of every sentence used.
- Example sentences that Claude Sonnet 5.5 wrote for the entries without a
  Tatoeba sentence, accepted by the example review
  (tools/courses/generated.jsonl), under CC BY-SA 4.0 with the courses.
- Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org): pronunciation recordings
  found through Wiktionary and Lingua Libre (https://lingualibre.org). The
  courses hold only the file names; the recordings stay on Commons, where the
  page of each file names its author and licence (mostly CC BY-SA).

The starter course in tests/fixtures/courses was written for VocabBot and is
licensed under CC BY-SA 4.0.

